"In the lands along the upper Niger and Senegal rivers, people mined gold. Far to the north, in the middle of the Sahara, salt was dug from the ground. Caravans of camels crossed the Sahara between the two, carrying salt south and gold north, and towns grew up where the routes began and ended. The first great trading empire was Ghana, called Wagadu by its rulers. Its Soninke people acted as middlemen between the salt traders of the north and the producers of gold and ivory to the south, and the king taxed the trade, especially gold. Its last capital, Kumbi, had two towns: one for the king and one for Muslim traders. Before 1240 it was the greatest city of western Africa. About 1235 Sundiata Keita founded the empire of Mali. Much of what we know about him comes from oral traditions kept by griots, who were hereditary historians, poets, and spokespeople for the king. Mali reached its height under Mansa Musa, whose pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, with a caravan of some 60,000 people and great amounts of gold, made Mali famous in North Africa and Europe. Mali's cities became centers of trade and learning. At Timbuktu, where the desert meets the Niger, merchants exchanged gold and enslaved people for salt, North African cloth, and horses. Enslaved people were bought and sold across the Sahara, and some served in the king's administration; historians study this too. In 1352 the traveler Ibn Battuta crossed the Sahara from Sijilmasa and described Taghaza, a village whose houses were built of blocks of salt. In the 15th and 16th centuries, the Songhai empire, centered on Gao, became a great trading state of West Africa. Timbuktu reached the height of its trade and scholarship under the Askia rulers, from 1493 to 1591. In 1591 an army from Morocco took Timbuktu, and the city declined. On the east coast, Swahili towns such as Mogadishu and Kilwa traded across the Indian Ocean with ports in Arabia, Persia, and India, and Chinese porcelain reached them too. Kilwa grew rich by controlling the trade with Sofala, where gold arrived from the lands near Great Zimbabwe. In 1505 Portuguese ships seized Sofala and briefly held Kilwa, trying to take over the gold trade.": "6fa907bb0b92",
